<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1795 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Heil dir im Siegerkranz</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>The anthem of the kings of Prussia and then the German emperors, sung to the same tune as Britain's God Save the King.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Heinrich Harries; adapted by Balthasar Gerhard Schumacher</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>anonymous (the melody of &quot;God Save the King&quot;)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>The Kingdom of Prussia; later the German Empire</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Prussia</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>words 1790, adapted 1793; royal anthem of Prussia from 1795</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Heinrich Harries (1790), adapted by Balthasar Gerhard Schumacher (d. 1805) in 1793; melody of &quot;God Save the King&quot;)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The words began as a song by Heinrich Harries, written in 1790 in honour of King Christian VII of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. In 1793 Balthasar Gerhard Schumacher (1755–1805) adapted Harries's text for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, shortening it and putting &quot;king&quot; where the Danish king's name had stood. From 1795 it was the royal anthem of Prussia, sung to the melody of the British anthem &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a>&quot;.</p><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> was proclaimed in 1871, the song stayed the Prussian royal anthem and came to be used for the empire too, with &quot;emperor&quot; in place of &quot;king&quot;. It is often called the national anthem of the German Empire, but the empire never had an official anthem; &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heil-dir-im-siegerkranz">Heil dir im Siegerkranz</a>&quot; and &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/die-wacht-am-rhein">Die Wacht am Rhein</a>&quot; both served as unofficial ones. Because it praised a Prussian monarch and borrowed a British tune, it never became popular across all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>: most German nationalists did not take to it, and southern states such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and Württemberg never recognised it. Bavaria had its own royal hymn, sung to the same British melody.</p><p>The emperor the song named was at first William I, who reigned until 1888. His son Frederick III reigned for only 99 days and was followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a>, whose frequent journeys led to a joke of the time that the title had become &quot;Heil dir im Sonderzug&quot; (&quot;Hail to thee in thy royal train&quot;). After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> began in 1914, Hugo Kaun set the text to new music to remove its likeness to &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a>&quot;.</p><p>At the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> was overthrown, and its successor, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a>, adopted &quot;Das Lied der Deutschen&quot; as its national anthem.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A hymn of praise to the monarch, first the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and later the German emperor, hailing him as a victor and wishing him and his realm well. The mood is stately and loyal, in the manner of the British anthem whose tune it borrowed.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">German</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">German</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Heil dir im Siegerkranz,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hail to you in the victor’s wreath,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Herrscher des Vaterlands!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>ruler of the fatherland!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Heil, Kaiser, dir!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hail, Emperor, to you!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Fühl in des Thrones Glanz</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Feel in the splendour of the throne</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">die hohe Wonne ganz,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the high delight in full</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Liebling des Volks zu sein!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>of being the people’s darling!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Heil, Kaiser, dir!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hail, Emperor, to you!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Nicht Ross und Reisige</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Not horses and mounted men</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">sichern die steile Höh,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>make safe the steep height</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">wo Fürsten stehn:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>where princes stand:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Liebe des Vaterlands,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>love of the fatherland,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Liebe des freien Manns</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>love of the free man</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">gründet den Herrscherthron</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>founds the ruler’s throne</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">wie Fels im Meer.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>like a rock in the sea.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Heilige Flamme, glüh,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Holy flame, glow,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">glüh und erlösche nie</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>glow and never go out</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">fürs Vaterland!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for the fatherland!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Wir alle stehen dann</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Then we shall all stand</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">mutig für einen Mann,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>bravely as one man,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">kämpfen und bluten gern</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and gladly fight and bleed</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">für Thron und Reich!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for throne and empire!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Handlung und Wissenschaft</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>May trade and learning</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">hebe mit Mut und Kraft</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>lift with courage and strength</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">ihr Haupt empor!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>their heads on high!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Krieger- und Heldenthat</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>May the deeds of warriors and heroes</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">finde ihr Lorbeerblatt</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>find their laurel leaf</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">treu aufgehoben dort</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>faithfully kept there</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">an deinem Thron!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>at your throne!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Sei, Kaiser Wilhelm, hier</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Be, Emperor Wilhelm, here</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">lang deines Volkes Zier,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>long your people’s ornament,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">der Menschheit Stolz!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the pride of mankind!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Fühl in des Thrones Glanz</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Feel in the splendour of the throne</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">die hohe Wonne ganz,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the high delight in full</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Liebling des Volks zu sein!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>of being the people’s darling!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Heil, Kaiser, dir!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hail, Emperor, to you!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Heinrich Harries (1790), adapted by Balthasar Gerhard Schumacher (1793); the imperial version, as quoted in German Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heil_dir_im_Siegerkranz" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (3)</summary><ul><li>The text is the version sung in the German Empire after 1871, addressed to the Emperor (Kaiser); Harries wrote for the King of Denmark in 1790 and Schumacher’s Prussian version of 1793 addressed the King.</li><li>Line 2.1: Reisige, mounted men-at-arms, an old word.</li><li>Line 4.1: Handlung, here trade or commerce.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
